---
# The `default` BackupTarget, B2's. longhorn-manager creates the object with an empty URL, and this
# render declares only the three fields the repo governs, so Flux's server-side apply never takes
# a field the manager writes (`syncRequestedAt`, the rest of `spec`, and `status`).
#
# `default` stays B2's, so the routing lists in the `longhorn-reconcile` role's defaults are the
# only thing that decides where a volume goes (ADR-0007).
#
# Declarative in BOTH directions: `k3s_longhorn_backup_armed: false` renders a BLANK URL, and Flux
# enforces the blank on every reconcile. Arming unconditionally would silently resurrect a
# deliberately blanked target and restart the B2 transaction-cap death spiral. A blank is the
# incident lever that stops all backupstore S3 traffic, so it survives every apply until the flag
# returns to true. The flag's comment in defaults/main.yml has the spiral's mechanism.
#
# The URL holds one placeholder, `${longhorn_b2_bucket}`, and R2's rule 3 admits it in a
# BackupTarget through `PLACEHOLDER_KIND_EXEMPT`
# (scripts/renderer/renderer_lib/secret_checks.py). The region is a plain variable.
apiVersion: longhorn.io/v1beta2
kind: BackupTarget
metadata:
  name: {{ 'b2_target' | longhorn_backup_name }}
  namespace: longhorn-system
spec:
  backupTargetURL: "{% if k3s_longhorn_backup_armed | bool %}s3://{{ longhorn_b2_bucket }}@{{ k3s_longhorn_b2_region }}/{{ k3s_longhorn_backup_prefix }}{% endif %}"
  credentialSecret: longhorn-b2
  # Seconds, rendered as the duration string the CR wants. Rate-limits the only recurring B2
  # caller in the cluster. See k3s_longhorn_backupstore_poll_interval in defaults/main.yml.
  pollInterval: "{{ k3s_longhorn_backupstore_poll_interval }}s"
